          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            and specifically in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %em BV-BRST formalism
            is a tool in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            to handle the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            = succeed "-constructions" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                in the construction of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+phase+space"} reduced
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+reduction"} symplectic reduction
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
                (“Hamiltonian BV”)
          %p
            In either case the
            %em BRST-BV
            complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mi BV
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(P^{BV})
            is a model in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-geometry"} dg-geometry
            of a joint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+intersection"} homotopy intersection
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimit"} (∞,1)-colimit
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limit"} (∞,1)-limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
          %p Accordingly, the BRST-BV complex is built from two main pieces:
          %ol
            %li
              it contains in positive degree a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-complex"} BRST-complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
              which is the homotopy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+Lie+algebroid"}> action Lie algebroid
              ) of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
              (in Lagrangian BV) or of the group of flows generated by the constraints (in Hamiltonian BFV) – which is in general an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
              in either case – acting on configuration space
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %ul
            %li
              it contains in negative degree a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul-Tate+resolution"} Koszul-Tate resolution
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+locus"} critical locus
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              (for Lagrangian BV) or of the constraint surface (in Hamiltonian BFV).
          %h2#LagrangianBV Lagrangian BV
          %h3#ClassicalBVAsHomologicalResolutionOfReducedPhaseSpace Classical BV as homological resolution of reduced phase space
          %p
            The
            %em classical Lagrangian BV-BRST complex
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            is, under suitable conditions, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+resolution"} homological resolution
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+intersection"} homotopy intersection
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (this is the BV part) of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries+of+the+Lagrangian"} infinitesimal symmetries of the Lagrangian
            (this is the BRST part), and hence a homological model of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+phase+space"} reduced phase space
            of the Lagrangian field theory.
          %p A detailed introduction to the classical Lagrangian BV-BRST formalism is at
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} A first idea of quantum field theory
              , chapter
              %em
                %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#ReducedPhaseSpace"} 11. Reduced phase space
          %h3#HomologicalIntegration Quantum BV as homological (path-)integration
          %p
            We discuss here the interpretation of the
            %em quantum BV-complex
            as a homological implementation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            thought of as
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            (in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            We indicate how on a finite dimensional smooth manifold the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-algebra"} BV-algebra
            appearing in Lagrangian BV-formalism is the dual of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            in the presence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            and how, by extention, this allows to interpret the BV-complex as a means for defining (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"}> path
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            over general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces"} configuration spaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            by passing to BV-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"}> cochain cohomology
            \.
          %p
            (The interpretation of the BV-differential as the dual de Rham differential necessary for this is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Witten90"}> Witten 90
            ) (
            %a{:href => "#Schwarz92"}> Schwarz 92
            ). A particularly clear-sighted account of the general relation is in
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"} Gwilliam 2013
            ).
          %p
            Further
            %a{:href => "#PoincareDualityOnHochschild"} below
            we discuss the generalization of these relation in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild (co)homology
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#IdeaOfPathIntegralQuantization"} The idea of path integral quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#MultivectorFieldsDualToDifferentialForms"} Multivector fields dual to differential forms
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#TheQuantumMasterEquationAsClosureOfIntegralMeasure"} The quantum master equation: the path integral measure is a closed form
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#IntegrationOverManifoldsByBVCohomology"} Integration over manifolds by BV cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#BVQuantization"} BV-quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#PathIntegrationAndQuantumObservablesByBVCohomology"} Path integration and quantum observables by BV-cohomology
          %h4#IdeaOfPathIntegralQuantization The idea of path integal quantization
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is supposed to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            using a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_S
            which is thought of in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu_S(\phi)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\exp\left(\frac{i}{\hbar} S\left(\phi\right)\right) \cdot \mu(\phi)
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  \\phi \in X
                  \\,,
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            some other measure and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S : X \to \mathbb{R}
            the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            on the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            its value as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            of the system is supposed to be what would be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi f
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi Fields
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi Fields
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle f \rangle_S
                  \=
                  \\frac{\int_{\phi \in Fields} f(\phi) \cdot \mu(\phi)}{\int_{\phi \in Fields} \mu(\phi) }
          %p
            if the measure existed. Of course this does not make sense in terms of the usual notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            against
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
            since such measures do not exists except in the simplest situation. But there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomological
            notion of integration where instead of actually performing an integral, we identify its value, if it exists, with a cohomology class and generally interpret that cohomology class as the expectation value, even if an actual integral against a measure does not exist. This is what BV formalism achieves, which we discuss after some preliminaries below in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#IntegrationOverManifoldsByBVCohomology"} Integration over manifolds by BV cohomology
          %h4#MultivectorFieldsDualToDifferentialForms Multivector fields dual to differential forms
          %p
            If one thinks of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as an ordinary
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d \lt \infty)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_S
            will be given by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_S \in \Omega^d(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polyvector+fields"} polyvector fields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mo •
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu
                  \\colon
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\wedge^{d-\bullet} \Gamma(T X)
                  \\,,
          %p
            which is usefully thought of as reversing degrees. Under this isomorphism the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+differential"} deRham differential
            maps to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
            operator, the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-operator"} BV-operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\mu \circ d_{dR} \circ \mu^{-1}
          %p
            which interacts naturally with the canonical bracket on multivector fields: the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schouten+bracket"} Schouten bracket
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polyvector+field"} polyvector field
            for more details.)
          #TheDualBVComplexOfTheDeRhamComplexOnAManifolds.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              and for
              = succeed "a" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \in \Omega^n(X)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BV
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    BV(X, \mu)
                    \\coloneqq
                    (\wedge^\bullet \Gamma(T X), \Delta_\mu)
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
              induced on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
              by dualizing the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
          #BVComplexOfManifoldIsPoisson0.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schouten+bracket"} Schouten bracket
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BV
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BV(X,\mu)
              makes this cochain complex a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+0-algebra"} Poisson 0-algebra
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+BV+and+BD"} relation between BV and BD
          %h4#TheQuantumMasterEquationAsClosureOfIntegralMeasure The quantum master equation: the path integral measure is a closed form
          %p Observe that
          %ul
            %li
              %p if we think of
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    the measure
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                    as some closed reference differential form on
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %li
                  %p
                    the exponentiated action functional
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi exp
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mi ℏ
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mo )
                            %mo )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp\left(\frac{i}{\hbar}S\left(-\right)\right)
                    as a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+field"} multivector field
                    on
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %li
                  %p
                    the expression
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mfrac
                            %mi i
                            %mi ℏ
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi μ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp(\frac{i}{\hbar}S(-)) \mu
                    as the contraction of this multivector field with
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %li
              %p
                then the
                %strong BV quantum master equaton
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \exp(\frac{i}{\hbar}S) = 0
                says nothing but that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp(\frac{i}{\hbar}S(-)) \mu
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em closed differential form
            %li
              %p
                If we furthermore take into account that in the presence of gauge symmetries the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is not a plain manifold but the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} algebroid
                of the gauge symmetries acting on the space of fields, hence an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NQ-supermanifold"} NQ-supermanifold
                (whose Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra is the
                = succeed ")," do
                  %strong BRST complex
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\frac{i}{\hbar}S) \mu
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+over+supermanifolds"} integrable form
                in the sense of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+over+supermanifolds"} integration theory of supermanifolds
          %p
            This means that Lagrangian BV formalism is nothing but a way of describing closed differential forms on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} Lie infinity-algebroid
            in terms of multivectors contracted into a reference differention form. The multivectors dual to degree 0 elements in the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} algebroid
            are the so-called “
            %strong> anti-fields
            ”, while those dual to the higher degree elements are the so-called “
            %strong> anti-ghosts
            ”.
          %h4#IntegrationOverManifoldsByBVCohomology Integration over manifolds by BV-cohomology
          %p
            The following proposition about integration of differential
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              On the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of differential forms
              of compact support
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∫
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cp
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int \;\colon\; \Omega^n_{cp} \to \mathbb{R}
              is equivalently given by the projection onto the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
              by the exact forms, hence by passing to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              in the truncated
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(B^n) \to \cdots \to \Omega^{n-1}(B^n) \to \Omega^n(B^n)
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+of+integration+is+the+exact+differential+forms"} kernel of integration is the exact differential forms
            for details.
          %p
            This “integration without integration” is discussed in more detail at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_S \in \Omega^n(X)
            be any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BV
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  BV(X,\mu_S) \coloneqq( \wedge^\bullet \Gamma(T X), \Delta_{\mu_S} )
          %p
            be the corresponding dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            by def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheDualBVComplexOfTheDeRhamComplexOnAManifolds"}
            above.
          #ExpectationValueOfFunctionOnManifold.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C^\infty(X)
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
              with respect to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_S
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi S
                    %mo ≔
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi f
                        %mo ⋅
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi S
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle f\rangle_{\mu_S}
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\frac{ \int_X f \cdot \mu_S }{\int_X \mu_S }
                    \\,.
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi BV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-]_{BV}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
            classes in the BV complex
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BV
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BV(X, \mu_S)
          #ExpectationValueOfFunctionOnManifoldByBVCohomology.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi BV
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in BV(X,\mu_S)_0 \simeq C^\infty(X)
              the cohomology class of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              in the BV complex is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExpectationValueOfFunctionOnManifold"}
              times the cohomology class of the unit function 1:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi BV
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi BV
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [f]_{BV}
                    \=
                    \\langle f\rangle_{\mu_S}
                    [1]_{BV}
                    \\,.
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam 13, lemma 2.2.2
            ).
          %h4#BVQuantization BV quantization
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            as above and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BV
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BV(X, \mu)
            for the BV-complex def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheDualBVComplexOfTheDeRhamComplexOnAManifolds"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \in \Omega^n(X)
          #ShiftInBVDifferentialOnManifoldDueToFunctional.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in C^\infty(X)
              then the BV-complex induced via def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheDualBVComplexOfTheDeRhamComplexOnAManifolds"}
              by the volume form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi S
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mi ℏ
                      %mi S
                      %mo )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mu_S \coloneqq \exp\left(\frac{1}{\hbar} S\right) \cdot \mu
            %p
              (for any constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              to be read as
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              itself by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi μ
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi ℏ
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi S
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_{\mu_S}
                    \=
                    \\Delta_\mu + \frac{1}{\hbar}\iota_{d S}
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota_{d S} : \wedge^\bullet \Gamma(T X) \to \wedge^{\bullet-1} \Gamma(T X)
              is the operation of acting with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
          #ClassicalBVComplexOnManifoldAsDerivedCriticalLocus.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p The complex
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi BV
                      %mi cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    BV_{cl}(X, S)
                    \\coloneqq
                    (\wedge^\bullet \Gamma(T X), \iota_{d S})
            %p
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
              of the function
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            By the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
          #ClassicalAndQuantumBVComplexOverManifold.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ShiftInBVDifferentialOnManifoldDueToFunctional"}
              and prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalBVComplexOnManifoldAsDerivedCriticalLocus"}
              together say that the BV-complex of a manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              for a volume form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_S
              shifted from a background volume form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              by a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mi ℏ
                      %mi S
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp\left(\frac{1}{\hbar} S\right)
              is an
              = succeed "-deformation" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              by a contribution of the background volume form
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %p
              We call
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\wedge^\bullet \Gamma(T X), \iota_{d S})
              the
              %strong classical BV complex
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi S
                    %mo +
                    %mi ℏ
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\wedge^\bullet \Gamma(T X), \iota_{d S} + \hbar \Delta_{\mu} )
              the
              %strong quantum BV complex
              of the manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              equipped with the function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and the voume form
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
          %p The crucial idea now is the following.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              %strong (central idea of BV quantization)
            %p
              In the above discussion of BV complexes over finite-dimensional manifolds, the construction of the
              %strong classical BV complex
              in remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalAndQuantumBVComplexOverManifold"}
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
              directly makes sense in great generality for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              defined on spaces of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
              more general than finite-dimensional
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "differential+cohesive+infinity-topos#CriticalLocus"} differential cohesive infinity-topos – critical locus
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
              by def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheDualBVComplexOfTheDeRhamComplexOnAManifolds"}
              breaks down as soon as the space of fields is no longer a finite dimensional manifold, hence breaks down for all but the most degenerate
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalAndQuantumBVComplexOverManifold"}
              we may instead think of the quantum BV complex as a certain
              %strong deformation
              of the classical BV complex, and
              %em that
              notion continues to make sense in full generality.
            %p
              And once such a deformation of a critical locus has been obtained, we may read prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExpectationValueOfFunctionOnManifoldByBVCohomology"}
              the other way round and regard the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              of the deformed complex as the
              %em definition
              of quantum expectation values of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
          %p
            See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Park"}> Park, 2.1
            )
          %p In order to implement this idea, we need to axiomatize those properties of classical BV complexes and their quantum deformation as above which we demand to be preserved by the generalization away from finite dimensional manifolds. This is what the following definitions do.
          #ClassicalBVComplexAsPoisson0Algebra.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong classical BV complex
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
              equipped with the structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+0-algebra"} Poisson 0-algebra
          #BeilinsonDrinfeldAlgebra.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong quantum BV complex
              or
              %strong Beilinson-Drinfeld algebra
              is a
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+algebra"} graded algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              over the ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} [ [ \hbar ] ]
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              in a formal constant
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+0-algebra"} Poisson bracket
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
              of degree 1 and with an operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \colon A \to A
              of degree 1 which satisfies:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^2 = 0
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi b
                        %mo +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo +
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta( a b) = (\Delta a) b  + (-1)^{\vert a\vert} a (\Delta b) + \hbar \{a,b\}
                  for all homogenous elements
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in A
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam 2013
            ) this is def. 2.2.5.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Drinfeld+algebra"} Beilinson-Drinfeld algebra
              is
              %em not
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar \{-,-\}
              measures the failure for the differential to satisfy the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leibniz+rule"} Leibniz rule
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              is
              %em not
              an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %p
              In this respect the notion of BV-quantization via BD-algebras differs from other traditional notions of BV-quantization, where one demands the quantum BV-complex to be a noncommutative dg-algebra deformation of the classical BV complex. But instead the BD-algebras induced by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
              and varying over open subsets of
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
              and
              %em that
              encodes the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra+of+observables"} factorization algebra of observables
              (see there for more).
          %p But:
          #ClassicalLimitOfBeilinsonDrinfeldAlgebra.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\hbar
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Drinfeld+algebra"} Beilinson-Drinfeld algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+limit"} classical limit
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras"} tensor product of algebras
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi ℏ
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A_{\hbar = 0}
                    \\coloneqq
                    A_\hbar \otimes_{\mathbb{R}[ [ \hbar ] ]} \mathbb{R}
            %p
              hence the result of setting the formal parameter
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"}> Planck's constant
              ”) to 0.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              The classical limit of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Drinfeld+algebra"} Beilinson-Drinfeld algebra
              is canonically a classical BV-complex, def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalBVComplexAsPoisson0Algebra"}
          #BVQuantizationByBDAlgebra.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\hbar = 0}
              a classical BV complex, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalBVComplexAsPoisson0Algebra"}
              %strong BV quantization
              of it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Drinfeld+algebra"} Beilinson-Drinfeld algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\hbar}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BeilinsonDrinfeldAlgebra"}
              whose classical limit, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalLimitOfBeilinsonDrinfeldAlgebra"}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\hbar = 0}
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam 2013
            ) this is def. 2.2.6.
