          %h4#PathIntegrationAndQuantumObservablesByBVCohomology Quantum observables by BV-cohomology
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+BV-complex"} quantum BV-complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              are the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
              of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            %p
              Specifically, an observable is a closed element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              in the quantum BV-complex and its
              %em expectation value
              is its image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f]
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+BV-complex"} quantum BV-complex
              by def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BVQuantizationByBDAlgebra"}
              its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              is, by definition, a perturbation of that of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+limit"} classical limit
              BV complex, def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalLimitOfBeilinsonDrinfeldAlgebra"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+perturbation+lemma"} homological perturbation lemma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
              this yields
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick%27s+lemma"} Wick's lemma
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
              for computing observables. (
              %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam 2013, section 2.3
              ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              For local theories (…)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing+operator"} gauge fixing operator
              (…)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
              (…)
          %p (…)
          %h3#PoincareDualityOnHochschild Poincaré duality on Hochschild (co)homology and framed little disk algebra
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#HomologicalIntegration"} above
            duality between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+field"} multivector field
            may be understood in a more general context.
          %p
            Multivector fields may be understood in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+homology"} cyclic homology
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            the product of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S(-))
            with a formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vol
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} vol
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+homology"} cyclic homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            is picked, and interpreted as a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vol
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} vol
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S(-))
            is regarded as a cocycle in cyclic cohomology, hence as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+field"} multivector field
            whose closure condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \exp(i S(-)) = 0
            is the quantum master equation of BV-formalism.
          %p
            By the identification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            = succeed "with" do
              %br/
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+loop+space"} derived loop space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            encodes the rotation of loops. Accordingly, the resuling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-algebra"} BV-algebra
            has an interpretation as an algebra over (the homology of) the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+little+disk+operad"} framed little disk operad
          %p
            For certain algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            there exists
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi HH
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi HH
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau : HH_i(A) \to HH^{n-i}(A)
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#VanDenBergh"}> VanDenBergh
            ) and this takes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes+coboundary+operator"} Connes coboundary operator
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+operator"} BV operator
            (
            %a{:href => "#Ginzburg"}> Ginzburg
            ).
          %h3#nonperturbative Non-perturbative
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            enhancements of BV-BRST formalism cast in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"}> differential
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
              cohesive
              = succeed "-topos" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            theory:
          %ul
            %li#AlfonsiYoung23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+A.+S.+Young"} Charles A. S. Young
              %em Towards non-perturbative BV-theory via derived differential cohesive geometry
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15106"}> arXiv:2307.15106
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h2#HamiltonianBV Hamiltonian BFV – Homotopical Poisson reduction
          %p
            The following is a rough survey of homotopical Poisson reduction, following (
            %a{:href => "#Stasheff96"}> Stasheff 96
            ).
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{-,-\})
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \coloneqq C^\infty(X)
            be its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          %p Consider
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \subset A
            %li
              %p that is closed under the Poisson bracket
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{I,I\} \subset I
              %p
                (one says that we have
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+class+constraint"} first class constraint
                or that the 0-locus of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                is
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em coisotropic
          %p
            By the Poisson bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            acts on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong Poisson reduction
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is the combined
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                passage to the 0-locus of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/I
            %li
              %p
                passage to the quotient of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^I
          %p This may be achieved in different orders:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong Sniatycky-Weinstein reduction is the object
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi SW
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi I
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi I
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A_{SW} := (A/I)^I
                    \\,.
            %p
              The
              %strong Dirac reduction
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi Dirac
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A_{Dirac} := N(I)/I
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(I) = \{f \in A | \{f, I\} \subset I\}
              is the “subalgebra of
              = succeed "s\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              These two algebras are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi red
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi SW
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi Dirac
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A_{red} := A_{SW} \simeq A_{Dirac}
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Suppose a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              acts on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moment+map"} moment map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu : X \to \mathfrak{g}^*
            %p
              Let then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
              of functions that vanish on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi μ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu^{-1}(0)
            %p
              Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi red
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{red}
              is the algebraic dual to the preimage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu^{-1}(0)
              quotiented by the Lie algebra action: the “constraint surface” quotiented by the symmetries.
            %p
              In fact, if 0 is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+value"} regular value
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi red
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{red} := \mu^{-1}(0)/G
              is a submanifold and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi red
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi red
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A_{red} \simeq C^\infty(X_{red})
                    \\,.
          %p
            We now discuss the BRST-BV complex for the set of constraints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{-,-\})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi red
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{red}
            in the following sense:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                instead of forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/G
                we form the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+stack"} quotient stack
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X//G
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            %li
              %p
                instead of forming the intersecton
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X|_{I = 0}
                we consider its derived locus.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{T_1, \cdots, T_N\}
            be any finite set of generators of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
            on the graded algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \otimes Sym(V)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
            in non-positive degree and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(V)
            is its symmetric
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+algebra"} tensor algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul-Tate+resolution"} Koszul-Tate resolution
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)/I
          %p Then on
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \otimes Sym(V) \otimes Sym(V^*)
          %p
            (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*
            in non-negative degree)
          %p
            there is an evident graded generalization of the Poisson bracket on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*
            just the canonical pairing.
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{c^\alpha\}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost"} ghost
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\pi_\alpha\}
            for the dual basis on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %strong ghost momenta
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Henneaux, Stasheff et al.)
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+perturbation+theory"}> homological perturbation theory
              )
            %p There exists an element
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega \in A \otimes S(V) \otimes S(V^*)
            %p
              the
              %strong BRST-BV charge
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\Omega, \Omega\} =  0
                  , so that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi V
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo :
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\otimes S(V) \otimes S(V^*), d := \{\Omega, -\})
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
                  is
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi V
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi I
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        H^0(A \otimes S(V) \otimes S(V^*), d = \{\Omega, -\}) = A/I
                        \\;
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mrow
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi V
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        H^{\lt 0}(A \otimes S(V) \otimes S(V^*), d = \{\Omega, -\}) = 0
                        \\;
                %p
                  (which says that this is in non-positive degree a resolution of the constraint locus
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/I
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                  is a regular ideal (meaing that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  can be chosen to be concentrated in degree 1) or the vanishing ideal of a coisotropic submanifold, then the cohomology in positive degree
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mrow
                            %mo •
                            %mo ≥
                            %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi V
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi CE
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi I
                        %mo ,
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        H^{\bullet \geq 0}(A \otimes S(V) \otimes S(V^*), d = \{\Omega, 0\})
                        \\simeq
                        H^\bullet(CE(A/I, I/I^2))
                %p
                  is isomorphic to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebroid cohomology
                  of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
                  whose
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Rinehart+algebra"} Lie-Rinehart algebra
                  is
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi I
                        %mo ,
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A/I, I/I^2)
                %p
                  (which says that in positive degree the BRST-BV complex is a resolution of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+Lie+algebroid"} action Lie algebroid
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi I
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{I,-\}
                  acting on
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Oh-Park, Cattaneo-Felder)
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subset X
              is coisotropic, there is an
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet  \Gamma(N C)
              such that the induced bracket on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi red
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^0 = A_{red}
              is the given one;
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Schätz)
              The BRST-BV complex with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
              as its Lie bracket is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
              to the above.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+BV-BRST+complex"} local BV-BRST complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+BV-BRST+bicomplex"} variational BV-BRST bicomplex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+BV+algebra"} homotopy BV algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+L-infinity+algebra"} quantum L-infinity algebra
          %h2#References References
          %h3#ReferencesGeneral General
          %p A classical standard references for the Lagrangian formalism is
          %ul
            %li#Henneaux90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on the Antifield-BRST formalism for gauge theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schweigert/ws07/henneaux2.pdf"} pdf
          %p Similarly the bulk of the textbook
          %ul
            %li#HenneauxTeitelboim
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Teitelboim"} Claudio Teitelboim
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantization+of+Gauge+Systems"} Quantization of Gauge Systems
          %p considers the Hamiltonian formulation. Chapters 17 and 18 are about the Lagrangian (“antifield”) formulation, with section 18.4 devoted to the relation between the two.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebroid"} L-infinity algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+BV-BRST+complex"} local BV-BRST complex
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            is made manifest in
          %ul
            %li#Barnich10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glenn+Barnich"}> Glenn Barnich
              , equation(3) of
              = succeed "," do
                %em A note on gauge systems from the point of view of Lie algebroids
              %p
                M. Schlichenmaier, T Voronov, (eds.) XXIX Workshop on Geometric Methods in Physics, vol. 1307 of AIP Conference Proceedings, 1307, 7 (2010) (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.0899"}> arXiv:1010.0899
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3527427"} doi:/10.1063/1.3527427
          %p
            Formulation as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+AQFT"} homotopy AQFT
          %ul
            %li#BeniniBruinsmaSchenkel19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Benini"}> Marco Benini
              , Simen Bruinsma,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
              %em Linear Yang-Mills theory as a homotopy AQFT
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.00999"}> arXiv:1906.00999
              )
          %p
            Review in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
              , §5 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher geometry in physics
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+Mathematical+Physics+2nd+ed"} Encyclopedia of Mathematical Physics 2nd ed
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.07308"} arXiv:2312.07308
          %h3#ReferencesForLagrangianBV Lagrangian BV
          %h4#ReferencesForLagrangianBVForLagrangianTheories For Lagrangian theories
          %p The original articles are
          %ul
            %li#BV81
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Batalin"}> Igor Batalin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigori+Vilkovisky"} Grigori Vilkovisky
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauge Algebra and Quantization
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(81)90205-7"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(81)90205-7
            %li#BV83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Batalin"}> Igor Batalin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigori+Vilkovisky"} Grigori Vilkovisky
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantization of Gauge Theories with Linearly Dependent Generators
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Batalin"}> Igor Batalin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigori+Vilkovisky"} Grigori Vilkovisky
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Existence Theorem For Gauge Algebra
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joaquim+Gomis"}> Joaquim Gomis
                , J. Paris, S. Samuel,
                %em Antibrackets, Antifields and Gauge Theory Quantization
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9412228"}> arXiv:hep-th/9412228
                )
            %li#Park
              %p
                J. Park,
                %em Pursuing the quantum world
                (
                %a{:href => "http://cds.cern.ch/record/638963/files/0308130.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"}> Pavel Mnev
                \.
                %em Lectures on Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism and its applications in topological quantum field theory
                (2017). (
                %a{:href => "https://www.arxiv.org/abs/1707.08096"}> arXiv:1707.08096
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+S.+Cattaneo"}> Alberto S. Cattaneo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"} Pavel Mnev
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michele+Schiavina"} Michele Schiavina
                = succeed "," do
                  %em BV Quantization
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+Mathematical+Physics+2nd+ed"} Encyclopedia of Mathematical Physics 2nd ed
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.07761"}> arXiv:2307.07761
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Geometrical aspects were pioneered in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Schwarz"}> Albert Schwarz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+approximation"} Semiclassical approximation
                    in Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism
                %strong 158
                (1993), no. 2, 373–396,
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104254246"} euclid
            %li
              %p
                M. Alexandrov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Kontsevich"} M. Kontsevich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Schwarz"} Albert Schwarz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The geometry of the master equation and topological quantum field theory
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9502010"} hep-th/9502010
          %p A systematic account of the classical master equation is also in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Kazhdan"}> David Kazhdan
                ,
                %em The classical master equation in the finite-dimensional case
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~theojf/KazhdanNotes.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#FelderKazhdan
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Felder"}> Giovanni Felder
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Kazhdan"} David Kazhdan
                %em The classical master equation
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1212.1631"}> arXiv:1212.1631
                )
          %p
            Generalization from
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "-actions:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+group"} algebraic group
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Benini"}> Marco Benini
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Safronov"} Pavel Safronov
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
              %em Classical BV formalism for group actions
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.14886"}> arXiv:2104.14886
              )
          %p Other discussions include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An introduction to the Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0402057"} arXiv:math/0402057
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em From topological field theory to deformation quantization and reduction
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uzh.ch/fileadmin/math/preprints/icm.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                M. Bächtold,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the finite dimensional BV formalism
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uzh.ch/reports/04_05.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Carlo Albert, Bea Bleile,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%BCrg+Fr%C3%B6hlich"} Jürg Fröhlich
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky integrals in finite dimensions
                %a{:href => "http://eprintweb.org/S/article/math-ph/0812.0464"} arXiv/0812.0464
            %li
              %p
                Qiu and Zabzine,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Graded Geometry, Batalin-Vilkovisky Formalism and their Applications
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.2680v2"} arXiv/1105.2680
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            ) in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            is discussed (for trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            and restricted to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariant+observables"} gauge invariant observables
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-complex"} BRST-complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            in
          %ul
            %li#Hollands07
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalized Quantum Yang-Mills Fields in Curved Spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.3340"} arXiv:0705.3340
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in the functional approach to classical field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5112"} arXiv:1101.5112
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"} arXiv:1110.5232
            %li#Rejzner11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in locally covariant field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5130"}> arXiv:1111.5130
                )
            %li#Rejzner13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                %em Remarks on local symmetry invariance in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.7037"}> arXiv:1301.7037
                )
          %p and surveyed in
          %ul
            %li#Rejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"}> Kasia Rejzner
              , section 7 of
              %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
              Springer 2016 (
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-319-25901-7"}> web
              )
          %p
            Discussion for field theories with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            and going in the direction of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#CattaneoMnevReshetikhin12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"} Pavel Mnev
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Reshetikhin"} Nicolai Reshetikhin
              = succeed "," do
                %em Classical BV theories on manifolds with boundary
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1201.0290"} arXiv:1201.0290
              = succeed "," do
                %em Classical and quantum Lagrangian field theories with boundary
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0239"} arXiv:1207.0239
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative quantum gauge theories on manifolds with boundary
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01221"} arxiv/1507.01221
          %p
            A discussion of BV-BRST formalism in the general context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Renormalization+and+Effective+Field+Theory"} Renormalization and Effective Field Theory
          %p
            Relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            is made explicit in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"}> Owen Gwilliam
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"} Theo Johnson-Freyd
              %em How to derive Feynman diagrams for finite-dimensional integrals directly from the BV formalism
              (2011) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1202.1554"}> arXiv:1202.1554
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"}> Theo Johnson-Freyd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Star-quantization via lattice topological field theory
              %a{:href => "http://scgp.stonybrook.edu/events/event-pages/string-math-2013"} String-Math 2013
              (
              %a{:href => "http://media.scgp.stonybrook.edu/presentations/20130618_johnson-freyd.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The interpretation of the BV quantum master equation as a description of closed differential forms acting as measures on infinite-dimensional spaces of fields is described in
          %ul#Witten90
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A note on the antibracket formalism
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 5
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=91h:81178"} MR91h:81178
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1142/S0217732390000561"} doi
              %a{:href => "http://ccdb4fs.kek.jp/cgi-bin/img_index?9004090"} scan
          %ul
            %li#Schwarz92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Schwarz"}> Albert Schwarz
              ,
              %em Geometry of Batalin-Vilkovisky quantization
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9205088"}> arXiv:hep-th/9205088
              )
          %p
            This isomorphisms between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            and the complex of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polyvector+field"} polyvector field
          %ul#WillwacherCalaque
            %li
              Thomas Willwacher, Damien Calaque
              %em Formality of cyclic cochains
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0806.4095"}> arXiv:0806.4095
              )
          %p and in section 2 of
          %ul#CattaneoFiorenzaLongoni
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"} Domenico Fiorenza
              %em On the Hochschild-Kostant-Rosenberg map for graded manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uzh.ch/fileadmin/math/preprints/05-06.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A discussion in the general context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-algebras"} BV-algebras
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Roger
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+Roger"}> Claude Roger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gerstenhaber and Batalin-Vilkovisky algebras
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/AM/09-4/roger.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The generalization of this to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            on Hochschild (co)homollogy is in
          %ul#VanDenBergh
            %li
              %p
                M. Van den Bergh,
                %em A relation between Hochschild homology and cohomology for Gorenstein rings
                \. Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 126 (1998), 1345–1348; (
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/118786"}> JSTOR
                )
              %p Correction: Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 130 (2002), 2809–2810.
          %p with more on that in
          %ul#Ginzburg
            %li
              %p
                U. Krähmer,
                %em Poincaré duality in Hochschild cohomology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.gla.ac.uk/~ukraehmer/brussels.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ginzburg"}> Victor Ginzburg
                ,
                %em Calabi-Yau Algebras
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AG/0612139"}> arXiv
                )
          %p
            The application in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              B. Zwiebach,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Closed string field theory: Quantum action and the B-V master equation
          %p
            A mathematically oriented reformulation of some of this (in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            ) is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              %em The Gromov-Witten potential associated to a TCFT
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.northwestern.edu/~costello/0509264.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Here the analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+fundamental+class"} virtual fundamental class
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of surfaces is realized as a solution to the BV-master equation.
          %p
            The perspective on the BV-complex as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
            is indicated in
          %ul
            %li#CostelloGwilliam
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"} Owen Gwilliam
              %em Factorization algebras in perturbative quantum field theory – Derived critical locus
              (weblass=‘newWikiWord’&gt;Derived%20critical%20locus
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Derived%2520critical%2520locus"}> ?
              \&lt;/span&gt;))
          %p
            A clear discussion of the BV-complex as a means for homological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Gwilliam
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"}> Owen Gwilliam
              ,
              %em Factorization algebras and free field theories
              PhD thesis (2013) (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/GwilliamThesis.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p Related Chern-Simons type graded action functionals are discussed also in
          %ul
            %li
              M.V. Movshev,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Schwarz"} Albert Schwarz
              %em Generalized Chern-Simons action and maximally supersymmetric gauge theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.7500"}> arXiv:1304.7500
              )
          %p
            Lectures, discussing also the relation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+complex"} graph complex
            are
          %ul
            %li
              Jian Qiu,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Zabzine"} Maxim Zabzine
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to graded geometry, Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism and their applications
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1105.2680"} arxiv/1105.2680
              = succeed "," do
                %em Knot weight systems from graded symplectic geometry
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5234"} arxiv/1110.5234
              = succeed "," do
                %em Odd Chern-Simons theory, Lie algebra cohomology and characteristic classes
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.1243"} arxiv/0912.1243
            %li
              Klaus Fredenhagen, Katarzyna Rejzner,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"} arxiv/1110.5232
          %p Gluing aspects are in focus of the program explained in
          %ul
            %li
              Alberto S. Cattaneo, Pavel Mnev, Nicolai Reshetikhin,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative BV theories with Segal-like gluing
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1602.00741"} arxiv/1602.00741
          %p
            On extracting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebras
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            around a solutio, encoding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree+level"} tree level
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Grigoriev"}> Maxim Grigoriev
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Rudinsky"} Dmitry Rudinsky
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Notes on the
                  = succeed "-approach" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              %strong 190
              (2023) 104863 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08990"}> arXiv2303.08990
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geomphys.2023.104863"} doi:10.1016/j.geomphys.2023.104863
          %h4#ReferencesForNonLagrangianEquations For non-Lagrangian theories
          %p
            The whole formalism also applies to the locus of solutions of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                D.S. Kaparulin, S.L. Lyakhovich,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.A.+Sharapov"} A.A. Sharapov
                %em Local BRST cohomology in (non-)Lagrangian field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1106.4252"}> arXiv:1106.4252
                )
            %li
              %p
                D.S. Kaparulin, S.L. Lyakhovich, A.A. Sharapov,
                %em Rigid Symmetries and Conservation Laws in Non-Lagrangian Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0091"}> arXiv:1001.0091
                )
            %li
              %p
                S.L. Lyakhovich, A.A. Sharapov,
                %em Quantizing non-Lagrangian gauge theories: an augmentation method
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0612086"}> arXiv:hep-th/0612086
                )
            %li
              %p
                S.L. Lyakhovich, A.A. Sharapov,
                %em BRST theory without Hamiltonian and Lagrangian
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0411247"}> arXiv:hep-th/0411247
                )
          %p Section 4.5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gennadi+Sardanashvily"}> Gennadi Sardanashvily
              ,
              %em Advanced Classical Field Theory
              (2009) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.g-sardanashvily.ru/book09.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p This also makes the connection to
          %ul
            %li
              P. Olver,
              %em Applications of Lie Groups to Differential Equations
              (Springer-Verlag, Berlin) (1986)
          %p
            See also via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+L-infinity+algebras"} quantum L-infinity algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Doubek"}> Martin Doubek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Branislav+Jur%C4%8Do"} Branislav Jurčo
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%A1n+Pulmann"} Ján Pulmann
                %em Quantum L∞ Algebras and the Homological Perturbation Lemma
                Comm. Math. Phys.
                %strong 367
                (2019) 215–240 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.02696"}> arXiv:1712.02696
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-019-03375-x"} doi:10.1007/s00220-019-03375-x
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Branislav+Jur%C4%8Do"}> Branislav Jurčo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tommaso+Macrelli"} Tommaso Macrelli
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+S%C3%A4mann"} Christian Sämann
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Wolf"} Martin Wolf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Loop Amplitudes and Quantum Homotopy Algebras
                %strong 2020
                3 (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP07(2020)003"}> doi:10.1007/JHEP07(2020)003
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.06695"} arXiv:1912.06695
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              Sebastian Albrecht:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Formulation of Batalin-Vilkovisky Field Theories as Homotopy Lie Algebras
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.theorie.physik.uni-muenchen.de/TMP/theses/sebastian_albrecht.pdf"} pdf
          %h4#for_cftvertex_algebras For CFT/vertex algebras
          %p
            A class of “free” vertex algebras are also quantized using Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism, with results on quantization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BCOV+theory"} BCOV theory
            important for understanding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
          %ul
            %li
              Si Li,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vertex algebras and quantum master equation
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01292"} arxiv/1612.01292
          %h3#hamiltonian_bfv Hamiltonian BFV
          %p BRST formalism is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glenn+Barnich"}> Glenn Barnich
              , Friedemann Brandt,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"} Marc Henneaux
              = succeed "," do
                %em Local BRST cohomology in gauge theories
              %strong 338
              (2000), no. 5, 439–569,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://xxx.lanl.gov/abs/hep-th/0002245"} hep-th/0002245
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0370-1573(00)00049-1"} doi
          %p The original references on Hamiltonian BFV formalism are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Batalin"}> Igor Batalin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigori+Vilkovisky"} Grigori Vilkovisky
                %em Relativistic S-matrix of dynamical systems with boson and fermion constraints
                , Phys. Lett.
                %strong B69
                (1977) 309-312;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Batalin"}> Igor Batalin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Efim+Fradkin"} Efim Fradkin
                %em A generalized canonical formalism and quantization of reducible gauge theories
                , Phys. Lett. B122 (1983) 157-164.
          %p Homological Poisson reduction is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Stasheff96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"}> Jim Stasheff
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homological Reduction of Constrained Poisson Algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9603021"} arXiv:q-alg/9603021
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214459757"} Euclid
          %p
            Remarks on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            interpretation of BRST-BV are in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"}> Jim Stasheff
              ,
              %em The (secret?) homological algebra of the Batalin-Vilkovisky approach
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9712157"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            A standard textbook on the application of BRST-BV to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Teitelboim"} Claudio Teitelboim
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantization of gauge systems
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glenn+Barnich"}> Glenn Barnich
                , Friedemann Brandt,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"} Marc Henneaux
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Local BRST cohomology in the antifield formalism. I. General theorems
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104275094"} euclid
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=97c:81186"} MR97c:81186
            %li
              %p
                %em Basics of Poisson reduction
                (
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/07/poisson_reduction.html"}> blog
                )
            %li
              %p
                Alejandro Cabrera,
                %em Homological BV-BRST methods: from QFT to Poisson reduction
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/Charla_IMPA_BRST.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"}> Jeremy Butterfield
                ,
                %em On symplectic reduction in classical mechanis
                (
                %a{:href => "http://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/archive/00002373/01/ButterfieldNHSympRed.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                S. Lyakhovich, A. Sharapov,
                %em BRST theory without Hamiltonian and Lagrangian
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/hep-th/pdf/0411/0411247v2.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Florian+Sch%C3%A4tz"}> Florian Schätz
                ,
                %em BFV-complex and higher homotopy structures
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ist.utl.pt/~fschaetz/BFV-complex.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                MO:
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/30352/what-is-the-batalin-vilkovisky-formalism-and-what-are-its-uses-in-mathematics/32443#32443"} what is the BV formalism and its uses
          %h3#ReferencesMultisymplectic Multisymplectic BRST
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Sean Hrabak,
                %em Ambient Diffeomorphism Symmetries of Embedded Submanifolds, Multisymplectic BRST and Pseudoholomorphic Embeddings
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9904026"}> arXiv:math-ph/9904026
                )
            %li
              %p
                Sean Hrabak,
                %em On a Multisymplectic Formulation of the Classical BRST symmetry for First Order Field Theories Part I: Algebraic Structures
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9901012"}> arXiv:math-ph/9901012
                )
            %li
              %p
                Sean Hrabak,
                %em On a Multisymplectic Formulation of the Classical BRST Symmetry for First Order Field Theories Part II: Geometric Structures
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9901013"}> arXiv:math-ph/9901013
                )
          %p based on
          %ul
            %li
              I. Kanatchikov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On field theoretic generalizations of a Poisson algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9710069"} arXiv:hep-th/9710069
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on June  6, 2024 at 00:18:20.
